tute a number in the polynomial  \nand get zero. Then do it again and again  \nget zero and so \\(k\\) times... But on the  \n\\((k+1)\\)-st time the zero does not appear.From the mathematical folklore of\nMoscow State University1.1\\quad Matrices; basic factsSquare matrices give us the remarkable generalization of the notion of a\nnumber. Let us denote by Mat<sub>n</sub>(K) the set of all \\(n \\times n\\) matrices with*Department of Mathematics, The University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA 19104-6395 E-mail address: kirillov@math.upenn.edu--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMath480/540, TOPICS IN MODERN MATH. (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=2152246fb27cdb39092654586fd03fd7d78f39c4&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn592search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; What are numbers? ... It support the thesis that there is only one mathematics in ... substitute a number in the polynomial ... 6395 E-mail address: kirillov@math.upenn.edu\nMath480/540, TOPICS IN MODERN MATH.\nWhat are numbers?\nPart II\nA. A. Kirillov∗\nJan. 2008\nIn a role of numbers can occur not only elements of a ﬁeld or a skew-\nﬁeld. In this part I talk about other mathematical objects which are used\nas numbers. It is interesting that all of them had appeared ﬁrst in “pure”\nmathematics and later were used in mathematical physics and some other\napplications. It support the thesis that there is only one mathematics in\nwhich some domains have already found application and other not yet.\n1\nMatrices as numbers\nExaminer: What is a multiple root of a\npolynomial?\nUniversity entrant: Well, it is when we\nsubstitute a number in the polynomial\nand get zero. Then do it again and again\nget zero and so k times... But on the\n(k + 1)-st time the zero does not appear.\nFrom the mathematical folklore of\nMoscow State University\n1.1\nMatrices; basic facts\nSquare matrices give us the remarkable generalization of the notion of a\nnumber.\nLet us denote by Matn(K) the set of all n × n matrices with\n∗Department of Mathematics, The University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA 19104-\n6395 E-mail address: kirillov@math.upenn.edu\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1\n1 (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=ac37650a2d891a8470438972a8b516a2ed5c626e&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn592search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; What are numbers? ... The aim of this course is to show, what meaning has the notionof number in modern mathematics; tell about the problems arising ... 6395 E-mail address: kirillov@math.upenn.edu\nMath480/540, TOPICS IN MODERN MATH.\nWhat are numbers?\nPart I\nA.A.Kirillov\nDec. 2007\nAbstract\nThe aim of this course is to show, what meaning has the notion\nof number in modern mathematics; tell about the problems arising\nin connection with diﬀerent understanding of numbers and how these\nproblems are being solved. Of course, I can explain only ﬁrst steps of\ncorresponding theories. For those who want to know more, I indicate\nthe appropriate literature.\nPreface\nThe “muzhiks” near Vyatka lived badly. But\nthey did not know it and believed that they live\nwell, not worse than the others\nA.Krupin, “The live water.”\nWhen a school student ﬁrst meet mathematics, (s)he is told that it is a\nscience which studies numbers and ﬁgures. Later, in a college, (s)he learns\nanalytic geometry which express geometric notions using numbers. So, it\nseems that numbers is the only object of study in mathematics.\nTrue, if you open a modern mathematical journal and try to read any\narticle, it is very probable that you will see no numbers at all. Instead, au-\nthors speak about sets, functions, operators, groups, manifolds, categories,\netc.\n∗Department of Mathematics, The University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA 19104-\n6395 E-mail address: kirillov@math.upenn.edu\n2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhat Are Quantities?: Australasian Journal of Philosophy: Vol 94 , No 4 - Get Access (https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00048402.2015.1130071)\nciteturn592search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.7 years ago; Crawled: last month; For a detailed discussion of the meaning and purpose of the context principle in Frege's Grundlagen, see Kim [Citation2011a].^{3} One of the objections that Frege [Citation1884: sec. 56] raised to the view of numbers as (higher-level) properties is that, when there are exactly m Fs and there are also exactly n Fs, it must be the m and n—not the properties of there being m Fs and there being n Fs—that are identical, since, in Frege's view, properties cannot bear the identity relation to one another. ... What Are Numbers?\nTo be more precise, ‘a is q in Q’ should be written as ‘Q_{q}(a)’, using ‘Q’ as an incomplete predicate (variable) to be supplemented with a quantity expression ‘q’.\n\n^{5} Or, to use the current scientific standard of the metre, (20) should read: a is 1 metre long =_{Df} a is as long as ‘the path travelled by light in vacuum during a time interval of 1/299 792 458 of a second’ (see http://www.bipm.org/en/CGPM/db/17/1/).\n\n^{6} It is a fair question to ask what ‘1 m ⊗ 3’ really means. More generally, since a quantity expression ‘q’ primarily occurs in the context ‘a is q in Q’, and expressions of the form ‘q ⊗ r’ are supposed to be quantity expressions themselves, their meaning should be explained by defining the phrase ‘a is q ⊗ r in Q’. It is beyond the scope of this paper to address this issue; but see the next note.\n\n^{7} Similarly to the case of quantitative multiplication (see previous note), there is the question of how to make sense of the quantitative addition symbol, ⊕, when it occurs in the context ‘a is q_{1} ⊕ q_{2} in Q’. Again, a discussion of this question is reserved for subsequent work. But, as a clue, consider addition of two counting numbers. Counting numbers primarily occur in the context ‘There are n Fs’, and the meaning of the phrase ‘There are m + n Fs’ could be explained by defining it to mean that there is a property G such that (there are m Fs that are Gs and there are n Fs that are not Gs). Likewise, we would need a definition of the phrase ‘a is q_{1} ⊕ q_{2} in Q’ which eliminates the quantity expression ‘q_{1} ⊕ q_{2}’ in terms of ‘a is q in Q’.\n\n^{8} Thanks to the audience at the 2015 Summer Meeting of the Korean Society for Logic. I am also grateful to the anonymous reviewers of this journal for helpful comments.\n\n* Eddon, M. 2013. Quantitative Properties, Philosophy Compass 8/7: 633–45.\n\n(Open in a new window)Google Scholar\n\n* Kim, J. 2013. What Are Numbers? Synthese 190/6: 1099–112.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhat Does It All Mean? (https://ideas.repec.org/h/spr/sprchp/978-1-4899-6014-6_15.html)\nciteturn592search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Now it is time to ask: What are numbers in the most general sense? ... \"What Does It All Mean?,\" Springer Books, in: The Mathematical Traveler, chapter 0, pages 247-262, Springer.\n\n# What Does It All Mean?\n\n## In: The Mathematical Traveler\n\n## Author\n\nListed:\n\n  * Calvin C. Clawson\n\nRegistered:\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe have come a long way in our quest for numbers. Beginning with the natural numbers, we have investigated their origins and expanded our ideas to include fractions, irrational, complex, and transfinite numbers. We have even speculated on how other species count. Now it is time to ask: What are numbers in the most general sense? This is really not a mathematical question but a philosophical one, and as such, we must be careful not to get bogged down in a quagmire of endless philosophical debate.\n\n## Suggested Citation\n\n* Calvin C. Clawson, 1994. \"What Does It All Mean?,\" Springer Books, in: The Mathematical Traveler, chapter 0, pages 247-262, Springer.\nHandle: RePEc:spr:sprchp:978-1-4899-6014-6_15\nDOI: 10.1007/978-1-4899-6014-6_15\n\n\n## Download full text from publisher\n\nTo our knowledge, this item is not available for download. To find whether it is available, there are three options:\n1. Check below whether another version of this item is available online.\n2. Check on the provider's web page whether it is in fact available.\n3. Perform a\n\n[Input][Button: search]\n\nfor a similarly titled item that would be available.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNumerical digit (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numerical_digit)\nciteturn592search16 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; - Kirillov, A.A.What are numbers?. ... The digital root is the single-digit number obtained by summing the digits of a given number, then summing the digits of the result, and so on until a single-digit number is obtained.\nNumerical digit\n## Digits in mathematics\n\nDespite the essential role of digits in describing numbers, they are relatively unimportant to modern mathematics. Nevertheless, there are a few important mathematical concepts that make use of the representation of a number as a sequence of digits.\n\n- Kirillov, A.A. What are numbers?.\n\n### Digital roots\n\nThe digital root is the single-digit number obtained by summing the digits of a given number, then summing the digits of the result, and so on until a single-digit number is obtained.\n\n- Weisstein, Eric W. Digital Root.\n\n### Casting out nines\n\nCasting out nines is a procedure for checking arithmetic done by hand. To describe it, let f(x) represent the digital root of x, as described above. Casting out nines makes use of the fact that if A + B = C, then f(f(A) + f(B)) = f(C). In the process of casting out nines, both sides of the latter equation are computed, and if they are not equal, the original addition must have been faulty.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Federation vs The UNSC | Sp